    "body": "#### vim-patch:9.2.1251: NFA engine does redundant work for some patterns\n\nProblem:  The NFA engine repeatedly traverses growing patch lists while\n          compiling optional atoms and decodes the current character\n          again for keyword and printable character classes.\nSolution: Prepend singleton patch lists and reuse the already decoded\n          character (Julien Voisin)\n\nCompiling optional atoms currently appends singleton entries to the end of\nthe unresolved patch list. For long optional-character expressions such\nas \\%[...], each append traverses an increasingly large list, resulting in\nquadratic compilation time.\n\nThe order of entries in the unresolved patch list does not affect matching\npriority, since priority is represented by the out and out1 branches of\nNFA_SPLIT states. Prepending singleton entries therefore avoids the list\ntraversal without changing matching behavior.\n\nIn a wall-clock benchmark that forced the NFA engine and compiled a\n4,000-character \\%[...] expression 100 times, compilation time decreased\nfrom 0.56 seconds to 0.01 seconds.  After the change, compiling expressions\ncontaining 1,000, 2,000, and 4,000 characters 1,000 times took 0.02, 0.04,\nand 0.09 seconds respectively, demonstrating approximately linear scaling.\nReproducing the benchmarks is simply a matter of running the following script:\n\n```\n/usr/bin/time -f 'n=4000 %e s' \\\n  src/vim -Nu NONE -N -n -es \\\n  --cmd 'set regexpengine=2' \\\n  -c 'let p = \"\\\\%[\" .. repeat(\"x\", 4000) .. \"]\"' \\\n  -c 'for i in range(99) | call match(\"\", p .. i) | endfor' \\\n  -c 'qa!'\n```\n\nThe matching loop also decodes the current input character before\nprocessing active states. Reuse that value for \\k, \\K, \\p, and \\P instead\nof decoding the same character again, like it's done everywhere else.\n\ncloses: vim/vim#21603\n\nhttps://github.com/vim/vim/commit/008d44279527f7d81ce96c6f428eb7f422390761\n\nCo-authored-by: Julien Voisin <julien.voisin@dustri.org>",
